(* Content-type: application/vnd.wolfram.mathematica *)

(*** Wolfram Notebook File ***)

Notebook[{
Cell["ORBIT Fixed-EH Original-Lagrangian Validation Notebook", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook turns the step-0 original-lagrangian validation into a stage-by-stage GUI workflow.

It is meant to be run in a fresh Mathematica 13.3 notebook kernel. Evaluate the code cells from top to bottom. Each stage stores its output in orbitNotebookState and returns a compact summary association so you can compare it against the expected checkpoint written below the cell.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
What this notebook checks:
- Start from the original LEFT-only EFT input.
- Rebuild the d <= 6 truncation from scratch.
- Apply the witness solution found earlier.
- Rebuild the fixed Einstein-Hilbert reference.
- Verify that the supported reduced sectors match GR.
- Verify that the only remaining reduced discrepancy is the quadratic {2,4} sector.
- Verify that this residual matches the projected Fierz-Pauli image of the validated higher-derivative field redefinition.

Important nuance:
- The raw unreduced full-reference shift does not directly reproduce the residual.
- The statement ORBIT proves here is the reduced / IBP statement.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Reading guide for the code:

- orbitNotebookState is just a shared Association used as a notebook-local cache. Each stage writes a small summary there so later stages can reuse the result without recomputing the explanation logic.
- canonExpr is the main xAct canonicalization step. Whenever you see it, the code is trying to put dummy indices and tensor expressions into a stable form before comparison.
- Quiet[..., ToCanonical::noident] suppresses a common xAct message that is expected in this workflow and is not, by itself, evidence of a failure.
- A projected coordinate vector means: write the answer in the chosen IBP basis of that sector and keep only the coefficient list. This is the cleanest way to compare two expressions modulo integration by parts.
- A reduced expression means: the expression after ORBIT has reduced it against the chosen sector basis and field-redefinition image. This is the physically relevant comparison used here.
- The code often stores both an expression and its coordinates. The expression is easier to read conceptually; the coordinates are easier to compare exactly.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 0. Setup", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This stage finds the repo root relative to the saved notebook, forces a Mathematica 13.3 check, loads the ORBIT packages, and prepares a shared state association for later stages.

If the version check fails, stop there and restart the notebook in Mathematica 13.3.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
What the setup code is doing, line by line:

- ClearAll[\"Global`*\"] removes stale symbols from earlier notebook experiments.
- NotebookDirectory[] gives the folder of the saved notebook. From there, the code walks upward until it finds orbit_fixed_eh_comparison.wl, which is used as a marker for the repo root.
- SetDirectory[repoRoot] makes later relative paths predictable.
- The explicit 13.3 guard matters because you asked that all validation be done only with Mathematica 13.3.
- inputMX points at the original EFT source.
- cacheDir tells ORBIT where to reuse precomputed sector data if available.
- basisOpts is the common option bundle passed into later reducer calls.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Start from a clean Global` context so old notebook symbols do not leak in. *)
ClearAll[\"Global`*\"];

(* Shared notebook-side state. Each stage writes a compact Association here. *)
orbitNotebookState = <||>;

(* Find the directory containing this notebook, then walk upward until the repo marker file appears. *)
notebookDir = Quiet @ Check[NotebookDirectory[], Directory[]];
repoRoot = NestWhile[
  DirectoryName,
  notebookDir,
  ! FileExistsQ[FileNameJoin[{#, \"orbit_fixed_eh_comparison.wl\"}]] &
];

(* Make relative paths like examples/... and cache/... resolve from the repo root. *)
SetDirectory[repoRoot];

(* Hard-stop if the front end is attached to the wrong Mathematica version. *)
If[! StringStartsQ[System`$Version, \"13.3\"],
  Print[
    \"ERROR: This notebook must be run under Mathematica 13.3. Current kernel: \" <>
    System`$Version
  ];
  Abort[]
];

(* Load the ORBIT toolkit and the fixed-EH comparison helpers used by the validator. *)
Get[\"graviton_basis_toolkit.wl\"];
Get[\"orbit_fixed_eh_comparison.wl\"];

(* Original EFT input and preferred cache directory. *)
inputMX = FileNameJoin[{Directory[], \"examples\", \"left_only_uv_2_eft_6\", \"LEFT_only_uv_2_eft_6.mx\"}];
cacheDir = If[
  DirectoryQ[FileNameJoin[{Directory[], \"cache\", \"left_only_uv_2_eft_6_cache\"}]],
  FileNameJoin[{Directory[], \"cache\", \"left_only_uv_2_eft_6_cache\"}],
  FileNameJoin[{Directory[], \"cache\", \"orbit_fixed_eh_cache\"}]
];

(* Common reducer options reused throughout the notebook. *)
basisOpts = {
  \"MaxDimension\" -> 6,
  \"CacheDirectory\" -> cacheDir,
  \"UseCache\" -> True,
  \"Verbose\" -> False
};

(* Save a compact summary so later stages and exported artifacts know how the session was configured. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"Setup\"] = <|
  \"NotebookDirectory\" -> notebookDir,
  \"RepoRoot\" -> repoRoot,
  \"KernelVersion\" -> System`$Version,
  \"InputSource\" -> inputMX,
  \"InputExistsQ\" -> FileExistsQ[inputMX],
  \"CacheDirectory\" -> cacheDir,
  \"BasisOptions\" -> basisOpts
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"Setup\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- KernelVersion starts with 13.3.
- InputExistsQ is True.
- RepoRoot points to the ORBIT checkout.
- CacheDirectory points to either cache/left_only_uv_2_eft_6_cache or cache/orbit_fixed_eh_cache.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 1. Witness and Helper Definitions", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This stage copies the exact witness substitution, the validated quadratic field redefinition, and the same helper functions used by the passing script validator.

This cell should finish without visible errors. Its main job is to seed the later computational stages.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
How to read the helper layer:

- witnessSubstitutions is the solved branch that turns the original EFT parameters into the specific point that should match GR up to the validated residual.
- witnessAssumptions is passed into FullSimplify so equalities are tested under the intended positivity assumptions.
- quadraticFieldRedefinitionFriendly is the human-readable delta h written with fixed dummy names.
- quadraticFieldRedefinitionExpr[x,y] is the same object packaged as a function that generates fresh dummy indices every time it is inserted into a larger expression. That avoids index-collision bugs.
- parameterDerivativeRules enforces the fact that M, kappa, and similar symbols are treated as constants, not spacetime fields.
- zeroExprQ and zeroVectorQWithAssumptions are the notebook's standard yes/no comparison tests.
- The comparison-builder functions at the end package sector-by-sector comparisons into Associations so the later cells stay readable.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Witness branch: this is the specific solution that should reproduce the fixed-EH claim. *)
witnessSubstitutions = {
  Scalar[M] -> M,
  Scalar[\\[Kappa]] -> \\[Kappa],
  Scalar[\\[Mu]bar2] -> \\[Mu]bar2,
  Sqrt[1/(M^2*\\[Kappa]^2)] -> 1/(M*\\[Kappa]),
  Sqrt[1/(Scalar[M]^2*Scalar[\\[Kappa]]^2)] -> 1/(M*\\[Kappa]),
  Sqrt[-(1/(M^2*\\[Kappa]^2*(1 + Log[\\[Mu]bar2/M^2])))] -> Sqrt[2]/(M*\\[Kappa]),
  Sqrt[-(1/((1 + Log[\\[Mu]bar2/M^2])*Scalar[M]^2*Scalar[\\[Kappa]]^2))] -> Sqrt[2]/(M*\\[Kappa]),
  Log[\\[Mu]bar2/M^2] -> -3/2,
  Log[Scalar[\\[Mu]bar2]/M^2] -> -3/2,
  Log[Scalar[\\[Mu]bar2]/Scalar[M]^2] -> -3/2,
  LambdaRefFinal -> 0,
  KappaRefFinal -> (4*Sqrt[3])/M
};

(* Assumptions used when a result is tested with FullSimplify. *)
witnessAssumptions = M > 0 && \\[Kappa] > 0 && \\[Mu]bar2 > 0;

(* Human-friendly version of the validated quadratic field redefinition. *)
quadraticFieldRedefinitionFriendly =
  (-3*PD[-i1][PD[i1][h[u, v]]])/(20*M^2) -
  (3*eta[u, v]*PD[-i2][PD[-i1][h[i1, i2]]])/(10*M^2) +
  (3*eta[u, v]*PD[-i2][PD[i2][h[i1, -i1]]])/(10*M^2);

(* Function version of the same delta h that refreshes dummy indices on each use. *)
quadraticFieldRedefinitionExpr[x_, y_] := Module[{j1, j2},
  j1 = DummyIn[TangentM];
  j2 = DummyIn[TangentM];
  canonExpr[
    (-3*PD[-j1][PD[j1][h[x, y]]])/(20*M^2) -
    (3*eta[x, y]*PD[-j2][PD[-j1][h[j1, j2]]])/(10*M^2) +
    (3*eta[x, y]*PD[-j2][PD[j2][h[j1, -j1]]])/(10*M^2)
  ]
];

(* Tell the symbolic derivative operator that the EFT parameters are constants. *)
parameterDerivativeRules = {
  HoldPattern[PD[_][M]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][\\[Kappa]]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][\\[Mu]bar2]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][LambdaRefFinal]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][KappaRefFinal]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][Scalar[M]]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][Scalar[\\[Kappa]]]] :> 0,
  HoldPattern[PD[_][Scalar[\\[Mu]bar2]]] :> 0
};

(* Collapse Scalar[...] wrappers to the standard symbols used in the witness branch. *)
normalizeScalarExpr[expr_] := normalizeMatcheteParameterScalars[expr];

(* Canonicalize an expression and then test whether it vanishes under the witness assumptions. *)
zeroExprQ[expr_, assumptions_ : True] := Module[{checked},
  checked = Quiet[canonExpr[normalizeScalarExpr[expr]], ToCanonical::noident];
  TrueQ[checked === 0] || TrueQ[FullSimplify[checked == 0, assumptions]]
];

(* Same idea as zeroExprQ, but for a whole coordinate vector. *)
zeroVectorQWithAssumptions[list_List, assumptions_ : True] := And @@ (
  TrueQ[FullSimplify[normalizeScalarExpr[#] == 0, assumptions]] & /@ list
);

(* Repeatedly remove derivatives of the constant parameters until nothing changes. *)
removeParameterDerivatives[expr_] := FixedPoint[
  Expand[# /. parameterDerivativeRules] &,
  expr
];

(* Build the first-order change in expr under h -> h + lam delta h and extract the lam coefficient. *)
firstOrderShiftFromExpression[expr_, deltaExprBuilder_] := Module[{shiftExpr, shiftedDelta},
  shiftExpr = Expand[expr /. HoldPattern[h[x_, y_]] :> (h[x, y] + lam dh[x, y])];
  shiftExpr = Coefficient[shiftExpr, lam, 1];
  shiftExpr = shiftExpr /. HoldPattern[dh[x_, y_]] :> (
    shiftedDelta = ReplaceDummies[deltaExprBuilder[x, y]];
    shiftedDelta
  );
  Quiet[
    canonExpr @ removeParameterDerivatives @ ReplaceDummies[shiftExpr],
    ToCanonical::noident
  ]
];

(* Compare one sector at the level of projected IBP-basis coordinates. *)
projectedSectorComparisonRecord[key_, inputSector_, referenceSector_, sectorData_] := Module[
  {basis, inputCoords, referenceCoords, differenceCoords, inputExpr, referenceExpr, differenceExpr},
  basis = sectorData[\"IBPBasis\"];
  inputCoords = If[AssociationQ[inputSector], inputSector[\"ProjectedIBPCoordinates\"], ConstantArray[0, Length[basis]]];
  referenceCoords = If[AssociationQ[referenceSector], referenceSector[\"ProjectedIBPCoordinates\"], ConstantArray[0, Length[basis]]];
  differenceCoords = inputCoords - referenceCoords;
  inputExpr = If[AssociationQ[inputSector], inputSector[\"ProjectedIBPExpression\"], 0];
  referenceExpr = If[AssociationQ[referenceSector], referenceSector[\"ProjectedIBPExpression\"], 0];
  differenceExpr = canonExpr @ LinearCombination[differenceCoords, basis];
  <|
    \"Sector\" -> key,
    \"InputProjectedCoordinates\" -> inputCoords,
    \"ReferenceProjectedCoordinates\" -> referenceCoords,
    \"DifferenceProjectedCoordinates\" -> differenceCoords,
    \"InputProjectedExpression\" -> inputExpr,
    \"ReferenceProjectedExpression\" -> referenceExpr,
    \"DifferenceProjectedExpression\" -> differenceExpr,
    \"DifferenceIsZeroQ\" -> zeroExprQ[differenceExpr, witnessAssumptions],
    \"DifferenceCoordinatesZeroQ\" -> zeroVectorQWithAssumptions[differenceCoords, witnessAssumptions]
  |>
];

(* Build the projected comparison table for every sector present in either reduction. *)
buildProjectedSectorComparisons[inputReduction_Association, referenceReduction_Association] := Module[
  {allSectors},
  allSectors = Sort @ Union[
    Keys[inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]],
    Keys[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]
  ];
  Association @ Table[
    With[
      {
        key = sector,
        inputSector = sectorLookup[inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector],
        referenceSector = sectorLookup[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector],
        sectorData = Which[
          AssociationQ[sectorLookup[inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector]],
            inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"][sector][\"SectorData\"],
          AssociationQ[sectorLookup[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector]],
            referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"][sector][\"SectorData\"],
          True,
            ComputeSectorData[sector[[1]], sector[[2]], Sequence @@ basisOpts]
        ]
      },
      key -> projectedSectorComparisonRecord[key, inputSector, referenceSector, sectorData]
    ],
    {sector, allSectors}
  ]
];

(* Build the reduced-expression comparison table for every sector present in either reduction. *)
buildReducedSectorComparisons[inputReduction_Association, referenceReduction_Association] := Module[
  {allSectors},
  allSectors = Sort @ Union[
    Keys[inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]],
    Keys[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]
  ];
  Association @ Table[
    With[
      {
        key = sector,
        inputSector = sectorLookup[inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector],
        referenceSector = sectorLookup[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector],
        sectorData = Which[
          AssociationQ[sectorLookup[inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector]],
            inputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"][sector][\"SectorData\"],
          AssociationQ[sectorLookup[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], sector]],
            referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"][sector][\"SectorData\"],
          True,
            ComputeSectorData[sector[[1]], sector[[2]], Sequence @@ basisOpts]
        ]
      },
      key -> Join[
        sectorComparisonRecord[key, inputSector, referenceSector, sectorData],
        <|
          \"DifferenceZeroQ\" -> zeroExprQ[
            sectorComparisonRecord[key, inputSector, referenceSector, sectorData][\"DifferenceExpression\"],
            witnessAssumptions
          ],
          \"DifferenceCoordinatesZeroQ\" -> zeroVectorQWithAssumptions[
            sectorComparisonRecord[key, inputSector, referenceSector, sectorData][\"DifferenceCoordinates\"],
            witnessAssumptions
          ]
        |>
      ]
    ],
    {sector, allSectors}
  ]
];

(* Convenience helpers for extracting the sectors whose differences do not vanish. *)
nonZeroProjectedSectors[comparisons_Association] := Keys @ KeySelect[
  comparisons,
  ! TrueQ[comparisons[#][\"DifferenceIsZeroQ\"]] &
];

nonZeroReducedSectors[comparisons_Association] := Keys @ KeySelect[
  comparisons,
  ! TrueQ[comparisons[#][\"DifferenceZeroQ\"]] &
];

comparisonSummary[comparisons_Association] := <|
  \"Sectors\" -> Keys[comparisons],
  \"NonZeroProjectedDifferenceSectors\" -> nonZeroProjectedSectors[comparisons],
  \"AllProjectedDifferencesZeroQ\" -> And @@ Lookup[Values[comparisons], \"DifferenceIsZeroQ\", {}],
  \"AllProjectedCoordinateDifferencesZeroQ\" -> And @@ Lookup[Values[comparisons], \"DifferenceCoordinatesZeroQ\", {}],
  \"DifferenceExpressions\" -> Association @ KeyValueMap[#1 -> #2[\"DifferenceProjectedExpression\"] &, comparisons],
  \"DifferenceCoordinates\" -> Association @ KeyValueMap[#1 -> #2[\"DifferenceProjectedCoordinates\"] &, comparisons]
|>;

reducedComparisonSummary[comparisons_Association] := <|
  \"Sectors\" -> Keys[comparisons],
  \"NonZeroReducedDifferenceSectors\" -> nonZeroReducedSectors[comparisons],
  \"AllReducedDifferencesZeroQ\" -> And @@ Lookup[Values[comparisons], \"DifferenceZeroQ\", {}],
  \"AllReducedCoordinateDifferencesZeroQ\" -> And @@ Lookup[Values[comparisons], \"DifferenceCoordinatesZeroQ\", {}],
  \"DifferenceExpressions\" -> Association @ KeyValueMap[#1 -> #2[\"DifferenceExpression\"] &, comparisons],
  \"DifferenceCoordinates\" -> Association @ KeyValueMap[#1 -> #2[\"DifferenceCoordinates\"] &, comparisons]
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"Definitions\"] = <|
  \"WitnessSubstitutionCount\" -> Length[witnessSubstitutions],
  \"WitnessAssumptions\" -> witnessAssumptions,
  \"QuadraticFieldRedefinition\" -> quadraticFieldRedefinitionFriendly
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"Definitions\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- WitnessSubstitutionCount is 12.
- The displayed quadratic field redefinition is
  delta h_uv = -(3/(20 M^2)) Box h_uv
               -(3/(10 M^2)) eta_uv d_a d_b h_ab
               +(3/(10 M^2)) eta_uv Box h^a_a.
- No warnings or failures should appear here.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 2. Load and Normalize the Original EFT", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This is the heavy front-loaded stage. It translates the original Matchete .mx input to xAct, keeps the finite part, truncates to d <= 6, and applies the witness solution.

This was the slowest part of the script run, about 206 seconds on the recorded passing run. Small timing differences are normal.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Why these four intermediate expressions matter:

- translatedInput is the raw xAct translation of the original .mx file.
- normalizedInput removes pieces that are outside the finite part convention used in the comparison pipeline.
- truncatedInput discards terms above mass dimension 6, because this is the truncation being tested.
- witnessInputExpr is the actual EFT expression we compare to GR: same truncation, but now evaluated on the solved witness branch and cleaned into canonical form.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Translate the original Matchete file into the xAct language used by ORBIT. *)
{translationTime, translatedInput} = AbsoluteTiming[LoadMatcheteToXAct[inputMX]];

(* Keep only the finite part in the normalization convention used by the validator. *)
normalizedInput = NormalizeMatcheteFinitePart[translatedInput];

(* Throw away all terms above mass dimension 6. *)
truncatedInput = selectTermsByMassDimension[normalizedInput, 6];

(* Apply the solved witness branch and clean the result into a stable canonical representative. *)
witnessInputExpr = Quiet[
  canonExpr @ removeParameterDerivatives @ (normalizeScalarExpr[truncatedInput] /. witnessSubstitutions),
  ToCanonical::noident
];

(* Store only the key checkpoints, not the full giant expressions. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"InputPipeline\"] = <|
  \"TranslationTimeSeconds\" -> translationTime,
  \"TranslatedExpressionTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[translatedInput],
  \"NormalizedExpressionTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[normalizedInput],
  \"TruncatedExpressionTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[truncatedInput],
  \"WitnessExpressionTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[witnessInputExpr]
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"InputPipeline\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- TranslatedExpressionTermCount -> 396
- NormalizedExpressionTermCount -> 260
- TruncatedExpressionTermCount -> 260
- WitnessExpressionTermCount -> 107
- TranslationTimeSeconds is typically of order a few minutes, not milliseconds.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 3. Build the Witness-Fixed EH Reference", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This stage rebuilds the fixed Einstein-Hilbert reference through d <= 6 with symbolic KappaRefFinal and LambdaRefFinal, and only then applies the witness substitution. That mirrors the corrected validation logic.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Why the substitution is delayed:

- We do not want to hardwire KappaRefFinal = 4 Sqrt[3]/M too early, because the reference builder is supposed to construct the symbolic GR template first.
- After that symbolic reference exists, we apply the same witness branch used on the EFT side. This keeps the comparison symmetric.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Build the symbolic fixed-EH reference through d <= 6. *)
{referenceBuildTime, referenceData} = AbsoluteTiming @ Quiet[
  ExpandFixedEHReferenceLagrangian[
    \"MaxDimension\" -> 6,
    \"LambdaReferenceValue\" -> LambdaRefFinal,
    \"KappaReferenceValue\" -> KappaRefFinal,
    \"EHSign\" -> -1,
    \"CacheDirectory\" -> cacheDir,
    \"UseCache\" -> True,
    \"Verbose\" -> False
  ],
  ToCanonical::noident
];

(* Apply the witness branch only after the symbolic reference has been constructed. *)
referenceExpr = Quiet[
  canonExpr @ removeParameterDerivatives @ (normalizeScalarExpr[referenceData[\"ReferenceExpression\"]] /. witnessSubstitutions),
  ToCanonical::noident
];

(* Keep the timing, term count, and original symbolic parameter placeholders for inspection. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"ReferenceBuild\"] = <|
  \"ReferenceBuildTimeSeconds\" -> referenceBuildTime,
  \"ReferenceExpressionTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[referenceExpr],
  \"ReferenceParametersBeforeWitnessSubstitution\" -> <|
    \"LambdaReferenceValue\" -> referenceData[\"LambdaReferenceValue\"],
    \"KappaReferenceValue\" -> referenceData[\"KappaReferenceValue\"]
  |>
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"ReferenceBuild\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- ReferenceExpressionTermCount -> 106
- The symbolic reference parameters shown in ReferenceParametersBeforeWitnessSubstitution are LambdaRefFinal and KappaRefFinal.
- ReferenceBuildTimeSeconds should be short when the cache is present.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 4. Build the Candidate Field-Redefinition Shifts", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
Here we compute two related objects:
- the first-order shift of the full witness-fixed reference expression itself
- the projected Fierz-Pauli image generated directly from delta h

The later stages will show that the projected Fierz-Pauli image matches the reduced residual, while the raw full-reference shift does not.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Conceptual distinction:

- fullReferenceShift asks: if I literally replace h by h + delta h in the full reference expression and keep only first order in delta h, what comes out?
- fpShiftTruncated asks the cleaner question relevant for the proof: what is the image of delta h under the quadratic Fierz-Pauli kinetic operator, truncated to d <= 6?

The fresh validation showed that the second object is the right one to compare against the reduced residual.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Literal first-order shift of the full witness-fixed reference representative. *)
{fullShiftTime, fullReferenceShift} = AbsoluteTiming[
  firstOrderShiftFromExpression[referenceExpr, quadraticFieldRedefinitionExpr]
];

(* Restrict that literal shift to the d <= 6 truncation. *)
fullReferenceShiftTruncated = selectTermsByMassDimension[fullReferenceShift, 6];

(* Independently compute the projected Fierz-Pauli image of delta h itself. *)
fpShiftTruncated = selectTermsByMassDimension[
  Quiet[
    canonExpr @ removeParameterDerivatives @ ShiftFromRedefExpr[quadraticFieldRedefinitionExpr[u, v]],
    ToCanonical::noident
  ],
  6
];

(* Compare the two shift constructions directly. *)
fullShiftMinusFPShift = Quiet[
  canonExpr[fullReferenceShiftTruncated - fpShiftTruncated],
  ToCanonical::noident
];

(* Also build the shifted reference expression, because later stages reduce it as a separate object. *)
shiftedReferenceExpr = selectTermsByMassDimension[
  Quiet[
    canonExpr[referenceExpr + fullReferenceShiftTruncated],
    ToCanonical::noident
  ],
  6
];

(* Store the key diagnostics: sector support, term counts, and whether the two shift constructions agree. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftBuild\"] = <|
  \"FullReferenceShiftTimeSeconds\" -> fullShiftTime,
  \"FullReferenceShiftTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[fullReferenceShift],
  \"TruncatedFullReferenceShiftTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[fullReferenceShiftTruncated],
  \"ShiftedReferenceExpressionTermCount\" -> expressionTermCount[shiftedReferenceExpr],
  \"FullReferenceShiftSectors\" -> Sort[Keys[SplitTermsBySector[Expand[fullReferenceShiftTruncated]]]],
  \"FullReferenceShiftMatchesFPShiftAfterTruncationQ\" -> zeroExprQ[fullShiftMinusFPShift, witnessAssumptions]
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftBuild\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- FullReferenceShiftTermCount -> 338
- TruncatedFullReferenceShiftTermCount -> 21
- ShiftedReferenceExpressionTermCount -> 127
- FullReferenceShiftSectors -> {{1, 4}, {2, 4}}
- FullReferenceShiftMatchesFPShiftAfterTruncationQ is False. This is expected and is the important subtlety.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 5. Reduce the Original EFT and Extract Canonical Normalization", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This stage reduces the witness-fixed EFT and extracts the canonical normalization needed to match the Fierz-Pauli sign convention used elsewhere in the pipeline.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
What canonical normalization means here:

- ReduceLagrangian gives a reduced answer, but the overall normalization of the quadratic kinetic sector can still differ from the standard Fierz-Pauli convention.
- kineticNormalizationDataFromReduction inspects the quadratic sector and tells us the multiplicative factor needed to bring it to the target sign.
- canonicalNormalizeReduction then rescales the whole reduced answer consistently.

Without this step, the later GR comparison would be polluted by a trivial overall normalization mismatch.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Reduce the witness-fixed EFT into ORBIT's sector-by-sector basis. *)
{inputReductionTime, rawInputReduction} = AbsoluteTiming[
  Quiet[ReduceLagrangian[witnessInputExpr, Sequence @@ basisOpts], ToCanonical::noident]
];

(* Read off the overall factor needed to match the standard Fierz-Pauli normalization. *)
canonicalNormalization = Append[
  kineticNormalizationDataFromReduction[rawInputReduction, basisOpts],
  \"TargetQuadraticSign\" -> -1
];

(* Apply that normalization if the extraction succeeded; otherwise keep the raw reduction for debugging. *)
canonicalInputReduction = If[
  TrueQ[Lookup[canonicalNormalization, \"SucceededQ\", False]],
  canonicalNormalizeReduction[rawInputReduction, canonicalNormalization[\"Factor\"], -1],
  rawInputReduction
];

(* Store the normalization data and the list of reduced sectors that now exist. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"CanonicalInputReduction\"] = <|
  \"InputReductionTimeSeconds\" -> inputReductionTime,
  \"CanonicalNormalization\" -> canonicalNormalization,
  \"ReducedSectors\" -> Sort[Keys[canonicalInputReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]]
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"CanonicalInputReduction\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- CanonicalNormalization[\"SucceededQ\"] -> True
- CanonicalNormalization[\"Factor\"] -> -1/48*(M^2*\\[Kappa]^2)
- CanonicalNormalization[\"TargetQuadraticSign\"] -> -1
- ReducedSectors should include the supported sectors {2,2}, {3,2}, and {4,2}.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 6. Reduce the Reference Objects", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
We now reduce:
- the unshifted witness-fixed reference
- the raw full-reference first-order shift
- the projected Fierz-Pauli shift
- the shifted reference obtained by adding the raw shift back into the reference

These reductions are what the later comparison cells inspect.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Why four separate reductions are kept:

- referenceReduction is the GR benchmark before any extra field redefinition is applied.
- fullShiftReduction tracks what the literal full-reference shift becomes after reduction.
- fpShiftReduction tracks the reduced image of delta h under the Fierz-Pauli operator.
- shiftedReferenceReduction tells us what happens if we actually add the literal full-reference shift back into the reference before reducing.

Keeping all four makes it possible to separate the physically correct reduced statement from tempting but false stronger statements about the raw unreduced reference.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Reduce the unshifted witness-fixed GR reference. *)
{referenceReductionTime, referenceReduction} = AbsoluteTiming[
  Quiet[ReduceLagrangian[referenceExpr, Sequence @@ basisOpts], ToCanonical::noident]
];

(* Reduce the literal first-order shift of the full reference representative. *)
{fullShiftReductionTime, fullShiftReduction} = AbsoluteTiming[
  Quiet[ReduceLagrangian[fullReferenceShiftTruncated, Sequence @@ basisOpts], ToCanonical::noident]
];

(* Reduce the projected Fierz-Pauli image generated directly from delta h. *)
{fpShiftReductionTime, fpShiftReduction} = AbsoluteTiming[
  Quiet[ReduceLagrangian[fpShiftTruncated, Sequence @@ basisOpts], ToCanonical::noident]
];

(* Reduce the reference after the literal full-reference shift has been added in. *)
{shiftedReferenceReductionTime, shiftedReferenceReduction} = AbsoluteTiming[
  Quiet[ReduceLagrangian[shiftedReferenceExpr, Sequence @@ basisOpts], ToCanonical::noident]
];

(* Save just the timings and sector support of the four reduced objects. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"ReferenceReductions\"] = <|
  \"ReferenceReductionTimeSeconds\" -> referenceReductionTime,
  \"FullShiftReductionTimeSeconds\" -> fullShiftReductionTime,
  \"FPShiftReductionTimeSeconds\" -> fpShiftReductionTime,
  \"ShiftedReferenceReductionTimeSeconds\" -> shiftedReferenceReductionTime,
  \"ReferenceReducedSectors\" -> Sort[Keys[referenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]],
  \"FullShiftReducedSectors\" -> Sort[Keys[fullShiftReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]],
  \"FPShiftReducedSectors\" -> Sort[Keys[fpShiftReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]],
  \"ShiftedReferenceReducedSectors\" -> Sort[Keys[shiftedReferenceReduction[\"SectorReductions\"]]]
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"ReferenceReductions\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- The reference reduction contains the ordinary GR-supported sectors.
- The full-shift reduction contains {1,4} and {2,4}.
- The FP-shift reduction contains the quadratic four-derivative sector {2,4}.
- The timings are usually around seconds to tens of seconds, not minutes.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 7. Compare the Original-Lagrangian EFT Against GR", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This is the core physics checkpoint before using the field redefinition image.

The supported reduced sectors should already match exactly after the witness substitution. The only remaining reduced discrepancy should be the quadratic four-derivative sector {2,4}.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
What is being compared here:

- buildProjectedSectorComparisons keeps track of coordinate differences in the IBP basis.
- buildReducedSectorComparisons keeps track of the actual reduced expressions and their coordinates.
- supportedSectorsUpTo[6] selects the sectors that belong to the ordinary GR comparison target at this truncation.
- The notebook deliberately stores both the supported-sector summary and the full list of nonzero reduced sectors, because they answer slightly different questions.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Compare EFT vs GR sector by sector, both in projected coordinates and in reduced expressions. *)
beforeShiftComparisons = buildProjectedSectorComparisons[canonicalInputReduction, referenceReduction];
beforeShiftReducedComparisons = buildReducedSectorComparisons[canonicalInputReduction, referenceReduction];

(* Summaries compress the full comparison tables into the few diagnostics we care about. *)
beforeShiftSummary = comparisonSummary[beforeShiftComparisons];
beforeShiftReducedSummary = reducedComparisonSummary[beforeShiftReducedComparisons];

(* Supported sectors are the ones that should agree directly with GR at d <= 6. *)
supportedReducedComparisons = KeySelect[
  beforeShiftReducedComparisons,
  MemberQ[supportedSectorsUpTo[6], #] &
];

supportedReducedSummary = reducedComparisonSummary[supportedReducedComparisons];

(* Pull out the surviving quadratic four-derivative coordinate vector explicitly. *)
quadraticDifferenceCoordinates = Lookup[
  beforeShiftComparisons[{2, 4}],
  \"DifferenceProjectedCoordinates\",
  {}
];

(* Store the core physical checkpoint of the notebook. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"BeforeShift\"] = <|
  \"SupportedReducedSectorSummary\" -> supportedReducedSummary,
  \"NonZeroReducedDifferenceSectors\" -> beforeShiftReducedSummary[\"NonZeroReducedDifferenceSectors\"],
  \"QuadraticDifferenceProjectedCoordinates\" -> quadraticDifferenceCoordinates
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"BeforeShift\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- SupportedReducedSectorSummary[\"AllReducedDifferencesZeroQ\"] -> True
- SupportedReducedSectorSummary[\"NonZeroReducedDifferenceSectors\"] -> {}
- NonZeroReducedDifferenceSectors -> {{2, 4}}
- QuadraticDifferenceProjectedCoordinates ->
  {9/(10*M^2), -9/(20*M^2), -3/(5*M^2), 3/(10*M^2), -3/(20*M^2)}\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 8. Compare the Residual Against delta h", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This stage performs the decisive match:
- extract the projected coordinates of the {2,4} residual
- extract the projected coordinates of the Fierz-Pauli image of delta h
- confirm that they match exactly

It also records the raw full-reference-shift coordinates to make the subtlety explicit.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
What each diagnostic below means:

- quadraticDifferenceCoordinates is the residual coming from the original-lagrangian pipeline.
- fpShiftQuadraticCoordinates is the projected Fierz-Pauli image of the explicit delta h.
- fullShiftQuadraticCoordinates is the quadratic part of the literal full-reference shift after reduction.
- fullShiftLinearFourDerivativeReducedZeroQ tests whether the extra {1,4} piece in that literal shift is physically trivial after reduction.

The expected pattern is:
- residual matches fpShift
- residual does not match the raw full-reference shift directly\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Read off the reduced {2,4} coordinates of the literal full-reference shift. *)
fullShiftQuadraticCoordinates = Lookup[
  sectorLookup[fullShiftReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], {2, 4}],
  \"ProjectedIBPCoordinates\",
  {}
];

(* Read off the reduced {2,4} coordinates of the projected Fierz-Pauli image of delta h. *)
fpShiftQuadraticCoordinates = Lookup[
  sectorLookup[fpShiftReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], {2, 4}],
  \"ProjectedIBPCoordinates\",
  {}
];

(* Test whether the extra {1,4} contribution in the literal shift is zero after reduction. *)
fullShiftLinearFourDerivativeReducedZeroQ = zeroExprQ[
  Lookup[
    sectorLookup[fullShiftReduction[\"SectorReductions\"], {1, 4}],
    \"ReducedExpression\",
    0
  ],
  witnessAssumptions
];

(* Package the decisive coordinate comparisons into a single small record. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftComparison\"] = <|
  \"QuadraticDifferenceProjectedCoordinates\" -> quadraticDifferenceCoordinates,
  \"FPShiftQuadraticProjectedCoordinates\" -> fpShiftQuadraticCoordinates,
  \"FullShiftQuadraticProjectedCoordinates\" -> fullShiftQuadraticCoordinates,
  \"QuadraticDifferenceMatchesFPShiftQ\" -> zeroVectorQWithAssumptions[
    quadraticDifferenceCoordinates - fpShiftQuadraticCoordinates,
    witnessAssumptions
  ],
  \"FullShiftLinearFourDerivativeReducedZeroQ\" -> fullShiftLinearFourDerivativeReducedZeroQ,
  \"RawFullReferenceShiftReproducesQuadraticResidualDirectlyQ\" -> zeroVectorQWithAssumptions[
    quadraticDifferenceCoordinates - fullShiftQuadraticCoordinates,
    witnessAssumptions
  ]
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftComparison\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- QuadraticDifferenceMatchesFPShiftQ -> True
- QuadraticDifferenceProjectedCoordinates and FPShiftQuadraticProjectedCoordinates agree and both equal
  {9/(10*M^2), -9/(20*M^2), -3/(5*M^2), 3/(10*M^2), -3/(20*M^2)}
- FullShiftQuadraticProjectedCoordinates -> {0, 0, 0, 0, 0}
- FullShiftLinearFourDerivativeReducedZeroQ -> True
- RawFullReferenceShiftReproducesQuadraticResidualDirectlyQ -> False

Healthy interpretation:
- the reduced residual matches the projected FP image of delta h
- the raw full-reference shift is not the right literal object to compare against that residual.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Stage 9. Final Summary", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
This cell compresses the whole notebook into a small conclusion table. A healthy run has the first four booleans equal to True and the final direct-raw-shift boolean equal to False.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
How to read the final booleans:

- The first four are the positive statements we want ORBIT to satisfy.
- The last one is intentionally expected to be False. It records the corrected understanding that the raw full-reference shift is not the right literal object to compare directly to the reduced residual.
- So a good run ends with four True values and then one False.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Final high-level pass/fail digest of the notebook. *)
orbitNotebookState[\"FinalSummary\"] = <|
  \"SupportedSectorsMatchGRQ\" -> TrueQ[
    orbitNotebookState[\"BeforeShift\", \"SupportedReducedSectorSummary\", \"AllReducedDifferencesZeroQ\"]
  ],
  \"OnlyReducedResidualIs{2,4}Q\" -> (
    Sort[orbitNotebookState[\"BeforeShift\", \"NonZeroReducedDifferenceSectors\"]] === {{2, 4}}
  ),
  \"ResidualMatchesProjectedFPShiftQ\" -> TrueQ[
    orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftComparison\", \"QuadraticDifferenceMatchesFPShiftQ\"]
  ],
  \"Extra{1,4}PieceReducesToZeroQ\" -> TrueQ[
    orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftComparison\", \"FullShiftLinearFourDerivativeReducedZeroQ\"]
  ],
  \"RawFullReferenceShiftReproducesResidualDirectlyQ\" -> TrueQ[
    orbitNotebookState[\"ShiftComparison\", \"RawFullReferenceShiftReproducesQuadraticResidualDirectlyQ\"]
  ],
  \"Conclusion\" -> \"From the original Lagrangian, the fixed-EH claim is confirmed in the reduced / IBP sense.\"
|>;

orbitNotebookState[\"FinalSummary\"]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- SupportedSectorsMatchGRQ -> True
- OnlyReducedResidualIs{2,4}Q -> True
- ResidualMatchesProjectedFPShiftQ -> True
- Extra{1,4}PieceReducesToZeroQ -> True
- RawFullReferenceShiftReproducesResidualDirectlyQ -> False

That pattern is the end-to-end statement we now trust.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Optional: Export This Notebook Run", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
If you want a reviewable artifact from your own notebook run, evaluate the next cell. It writes the in-memory state association to tests/artifacts/scratch/.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
This export is optional. It does not rerun the physics. It only saves the summaries already stored in orbitNotebookState so you can inspect or diff your notebook run later.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(* Destination for a reviewable notebook-side artifact. *)
exportFile = FileNameJoin[
  {repoRoot, \"tests\", \"artifacts\", \"scratch\", \"orbit_fixed_eh_original_lagrangian_notebook_run_output.wl\"}
];

(* Create the output folder if it does not already exist. *)
If[! DirectoryQ[DirectoryName[exportFile]],
  CreateDirectory[DirectoryName[exportFile], CreateIntermediateDirectories -> True]
];

(* Write the in-memory notebook state to disk. *)
Put[orbitNotebookState, exportFile];

(* Return a tiny success record. *)
<|\"ExportFile\" -> exportFile, \"ExistsQ\" -> FileExistsQ[exportFile]|>\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Expected result:
- ExistsQ -> True
- The exported association can be reloaded later with Get[exportFile].\
\>", "Text"]
},
Saveable->True,
WindowTitle->"orbit_fixed_eh_from_original_lagrangian_stage_by_stage",
PrintingOptions->{"PrintCellBrackets"->False},
